<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivangorod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1492">1492</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivangorod is a town in Leningrad Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> River opposite the Estonian city of Narva. Its fortress was founded in 1492 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, after whom it is named, facing the Livonian castle across the river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> held it in 1581–1590 and 1612–1704, and it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> between 1919 and 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivangorod.jpg" alt="Ivangorod" width="330" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vadim Tolbatov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ивангород.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ivangorod stands on the east bank of the Narva River, which flows along the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, 159 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and 218 kilometres east of Tallinn. It lies in Kingiseppsky District of Leningrad Oblast, directly opposite the Estonian town of Narva. The Tallinn to Saint Petersburg railway passes through it, and the Friendship Bridge, built in 1960, carries the road across the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chronicles first mention a New Village on the Narova River in 1470, and in 1473 the First Pskov Chronicle records Pskov and Novgorod envoys sent there to meet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a> opposite Rugodiv, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> was then called. The fortress was built in 1492, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>, as one of a series of fortifications on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. The site was chosen deliberately: the Grand Prince had asked about Baltic harbours in the 1480s, since the Baltic route allowed trade independent of foreign control. The town was meant to become the first seaport of the Russian state. Ivan was said to have blinded the architect so that he could build such a fortress for no one else.</p><p>The Swedes besieged and destroyed the first fortress in 1496, after which the Russians restored and enlarged it. With ten towers and mighty stone walls, it was the first Russian defensive work with a regular rectangular plan. German documents of the late 15th century called it a counter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> took the area, Ivangorod received town privileges and was administered as a Russian township until 1649, when its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> were ordered to move to a suburb of Narva. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stolbovo">Treaty of Stolbovo</a> of 1617 placed the area under Swedish sovereignty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> reconquered it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> in 1704.</p><p>Its military importance then waned, and from 1649 until 1945 it was administratively part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a>, remembered in the 19th century as the Ivanov side of Narva. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> took control of the whole of Narva, Ivangorod included, in January 1919; Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised this in the Treaty of Tartu of 1920. In 1922 the district had 7,000 inhabitants, of whom 42 percent were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. It was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1941 to 1944. In January 1945 the Soviet authorities made the Narva River the border between the Estonian and Russian republics, and Ivangorod passed to Leningrad Oblast. It gained town status on 28 October 1954.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ivangorod had a recorded population of 9,854 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2010. Its industries include textiles, food and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and the Narva Hydroelectric Station lies within its limits. It has thirty-three cultural heritage monuments of federal significance, all of them related to the fortress, which serves as a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivangorod” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sweden captured the fortresses of <b>Ivangorod</b>, Jama and Koporye during the Livonian War, confirmed by the Truce of Plussa in 1583, but returned them to Russia in 1595.</blockquote><a href="/state/swedish-ingria">Swedish Ingria</a></li></ul></section>
